│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ cem_smooth_field_path                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ This version uses a cross-entropy method (CEM) over a compact smooth-basis representation │
│                          │ of the path, instead of the old direct straight-line or coarse parameter sweep approach.  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The path is modeled as a linear baseline plus several endpoint-safe smooth basis          │
│                          │ functions: global bend, school-avoid bump, base-attraction bump, and asymmetric shape     │
│                          │ terms. A proxy multi-objective score is then minimized, balancing path length, school     │
│                          │ avoidance, base proximity, and curvature smoothness. CEM samples many candidate           │
│                          │ coefficient vectors, keeps the best elite set, and updates the search distribution        │
│                          │ iteratively. This gives a more global and flexible search than hand-tuned local rules,    │
│                          │ while still staying fast and deterministic.                                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Because the path is restricted to smooth basis functions and then lightly post-smoothed,  │
│                          │ it naturally produces a smooth line that bends away from the school region and toward the │
│                          │ base station region.                                                                      │
