╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_contour_bend                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The current approach has essentially no tunable optimization components: it uses a        │
│                          │ straight-line initialization only, with implicit fixed parameters of zero curvature       │
│                          │ control, zero obstacle avoidance, zero base attraction, and zero iterative refinement.    │
│                          │ That keeps F1 optimal, but leaves F2 and especially F3 weak.                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The new algorithm keeps the same input/output contract, but introduces a smooth           │
│                          │ parametric path and a lightweight adaptive optimizer:                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ - **Initialization**: start from the straight line between endpoints.                     │
│                          │ - **Shape terms**:                                                                        │
│                          │   - a **school repulsion bump** centered near the school X-position to push the path away │
│                          │ from `(40, 20)`;                                                                          │
│                          │   - a **base attraction bump** centered near the base X-position to pull the path toward  │
│                          │ `(70, -30)`;                                                                              │
│                          │   - a mild **global sag term** to help the path transition smoothly downward when useful. │
│                          │ - **Key parameters added/changed**:                                                       │
│                          │   - `repulse_amp`, `attract_amp`, `sag_amp`: amplitudes of the three shape components;    │
│                          │   - `sigma_school`, `sigma_base`, `sigma_sag`: spatial widths controlling how             │
│                          │ local/global each bend is;                                                                │
│                          │   - `coarse_steps`, `refine_steps`: two-stage search counts;                              │
│                          │   - `smooth_passes`: explicit smoothing strength to keep F1 low while improving F2/F3.    │
│                          │ - **Optimization strategy**:                                                              │
│                          │   - replaces the nonexistent optimizer with a **scheduled coordinate/local search**:      │
│                          │     - a coarse search over amplitude candidates,                                          │
│                          │     - then a refinement loop with shrinking step sizes.                                   │
│                          │   - this is more robust than a fixed-step gradient descent because it avoids unstable     │
│                          │ updates and works without requiring the grader’s exact differentiable objective.          │
│                          │ - **Selection criterion**:                                                                │
│                          │   - uses a surrogate objective combining distance, school proximity, base distance, and   │
│                          │ roughness, with stronger emphasis on avoiding the school and improving base coverage      │
│                          │ while preserving smoothness.                                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This should outperform the straight line by bending smoothly away from the school region  │
│                          │ and toward the base station region, reducing F2 and F3 without overly increasing F1.      │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
