│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adam_trajectory_refinement                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ This edit introduces a native Python Adam Optimizer that runs immediately after the grid  │
│                          │ search. The grid search is excellent at finding a robust global topological               │
│                          │ initialization (e.g., deciding which side of the school to pass), but it is limited by    │
│                          │ its rigid Gaussian basis shapes. By analytically differentiating the surrogate objectives │
│                          │ (`F1`, `F2`, and `F3`) with respect to every `Y` coordinate, we can treat the path as a   │
│                          │ completely flexible discrete spline (a rubber-band in an energy field).                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The `F1` gradient naturally behaves like a Laplacian smoothing operator, while `F2`       │
│                          │ repels the path from the school and `F3` attracts it to the base station. Adam fine-tunes │
│                          │ the coordinates for 150 steps, perfectly balancing the competing objectives and           │
│                          │ significantly lowering the final score.                                                   │
